\title{Sound Proof Automation for the Catalog:\\
Three Domain Tactics and Their Correctness Certificates}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-27}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present three custom proof tactics, each targeting a recurring proof pattern
in a large formal mathematics catalog, and we prove each one \emph{sound} --- that
is, incapable of certifying a false statement. The tactics are: \texttt{tropical\_simp},
a simplifier for the min-plus (tropical) semiring built exclusively from proven
rewrite equalities; \texttt{number\_theory\_decide}, a finite-case closer assembled
as a disjunction of sound primitive decision procedures; and \texttt{spectral\_bound},
an eigenvalue-magnitude estimator backed by a from-scratch proof of a weak
Gershgorin (absolute-row-sum) bound. For each tactic we establish a \emph{correctness
certificate}: a theorem whose truth guarantees the tactic's soundness. We then
demonstrate that each tactic attains its real utility only in concert with genuine
structural mathematics that decision procedures cannot supply. The flagship
illustrations are (i) an inductive \emph{scalar fold law} certifying tropical scalar
distribution over sums of arbitrary length; (ii) Pisano periodicity of the
Fibonacci sequence modulo $m$, proved by a paired two-track induction, with the
seed residues discharged by \texttt{number\_theory\_decide}, plus Fermat-style
congruences via reduction to $\ZMod\,p$; and (iii) the absolute-row-sum eigenvalue
bound with a symmetric-interval corollary for real eigenvalues. The recurring
thesis is a clean division of labor: sound automation handles the finite/routine
layer, while human-style reasoning supplies the structural core.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Formalized mathematics accumulates not only theorems but \emph{proof patterns}:
stereotyped sequences of steps that recur across many developments. Three such
patterns motivate this work.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Min-plus algebra.} Tropical (min-plus) computations --- shortest
paths, scheduling, dynamic programming, tropical geometry --- repeatedly require
normalizing expressions built from $\min$ and $+$ under a distributive law.
\item \textbf{Finite reduction in number theory.} A great many number-theoretic
claims over infinitely many integers reduce, after the right structural move (an
induction base, a quotient to $\ZMod\,m$, a residue seed), to a \emph{finite}
mechanical check.
\item \textbf{Eigenvalue localization.} Stability, convergence, and conditioning
arguments frequently need only a \emph{bound} on eigenvalue magnitudes, not exact
values.
\end{enumerate}

For each pattern we build a tactic that automates its routine kernel, and we prove
the tactic sound. Soundness here means: \emph{the tactic closes a goal only if the
goal is true.} We obtain soundness in two complementary styles. For
\texttt{tropical\_simp} and \texttt{number\_theory\_decide}, soundness is
\emph{structural} --- the tactic is assembled solely from sound components (proven
rewrite lemmas; individually sound decision procedures), so no false goal is
reachable. For \texttt{spectral\_bound}, soundness is \emph{certified by a theorem}
(\texttt{eigenvalue\_rowsum\_bound}) that the tactic merely applies.

\section{Preliminaries and notation}

We work over the reals $\R$, the naturals $\N$, and the integers $\Z$, and use
$\ZMod\,m$ for the integers modulo $m$ (a finite commutative ring, and a field when
$m$ is prime). The Fibonacci sequence is $F_0 = 0$, $F_1 = 1$,
$F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n$. For a square matrix $M \in \R^{n\times n}$ and vector
$v \in \R^n$, $Mv$ denotes the matrix--vector product $(Mv)_i = \sum_j M_{ij}v_j$,
and $\lambda$ is an \emph{eigenvalue} with \emph{eigenvector} $v \neq 0$ when
$Mv = \lambda v$.

A \textbf{tactic} is a metaprogram transforming proof goals. A tactic is
\textbf{sound} if every goal it closes is true. We use the following sound
primitives as black boxes: \texttt{decide} (kernel evaluation of a decidable
proposition); \texttt{norm\_num} (numeric normalization with arithmetic/primality
extensions); \texttt{omega} (linear integer/natural arithmetic); \texttt{nlinarith}
(nonlinear arithmetic over ordered fields); and \texttt{fin\_cases} (finite case
exhaustion). Each is independently sound.

\section{\texttt{tropical\_simp}: a sound min-plus simplifier}

\subsection{The min-plus semiring}

The \textbf{tropical (min-plus) semiring} equips $\R$ with $a \oplus b := \min(a,b)$
as addition and $a \odot b := a + b$ as multiplication. The distributive law
$a \odot (b \oplus c) = (a \odot b) \oplus (a \odot c)$ is the concrete real-number
identity
\begin{equation}
a + \min(b,c) = \min(a+b,\ a+c). \tag{3.1}
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{trop\_scalar\_min}]
For all $a,b,c \in \R$, $a + \min(b,c) = \min(a+b,\ a+c)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Case split on $b \le c$ vs.\ $c \le b$. If $b \le c$ then $a+b \le a+c$, so both
sides equal $a+b$; symmetrically otherwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{trop\_scalar\_min\_right}]
For all $a,b,c \in \R$, $\min(a,b) + c = \min(a+c,\ b+c)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Commute the additions and apply the previous lemma.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The tactic and its soundness}

\texttt{tropical\_simp} is defined as \texttt{simp only} over a fixed rewrite set:
the two distributivity lemmas together with the associative/commutative laws of
$\min$ (\texttt{min\_comm}, \texttt{min\_assoc}, \texttt{min\_left\_comm}),
idempotence (\texttt{min\_self}), and the additive monoid laws.

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of \texttt{tropical\_simp}]
Every goal closed by \texttt{tropical\_simp} is a true identity of real numbers.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\texttt{simp only [S]} rewrites the goal using only the equations in $S$ and closes
it when both sides become syntactically equal. Each member of $S$ is a proven
equality, so each rewrite preserves the truth value; a goal reduced to reflexivity
was therefore already true.
\end{proof}

The tactic is \emph{normalizing}, not complete: it cannot evaluate $\min(a,b)$ when
the order of $a,b$ is unknown. The law \texttt{min\_left\_comm} is essential ---
without it \texttt{simp} fails to canonicalize re-bracketed $\min$ trees.

\subsection{The structural core: scalars distribute over whole sums}

Model a tropical sum as a right fold of $\min$ over a list with base value $d$.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{scalar\_foldr\_min}]
For all $c,d \in \R$ and every finite list $\ell = [a_1,\dots,a_k]$ of reals,
\[
c + \foldr\,\min\,d\,\ell = \foldr\,\min\,(c+d)\,\big(\mapop\,(c + \cdot)\,\ell\big),
\]
i.e.\ $c + \min(a_1,\dots,a_k,d) = \min(c+a_1,\dots,c+a_k,\ c+d)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $\ell$. For $\ell = []$ both sides are $c+d$. For $\ell = a :: t$,
unfold the folds to expose $c + \min(a,\ \foldr\,\min\,d\,t)$; apply Lemma~3.1 to
split off $c+a$, then the induction hypothesis on $t$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Bridge: min-plus from max-plus by duality}

Min-plus and max-plus are exchanged by negation, $\min(a,b) = -\max(-a,-b)$.

\begin{proposition}[\texttt{minplus\_via\_maxplus}]
Identity (3.1) follows from the max-plus distributive law
$a + \max(b,c) = \max(a+b,a+c)$ via the negation duality.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Write $\min(b,c) = -\max(-b,-c)$ and $\min(a+b,a+c) = -\max(-(a+b),-(a+c))$, expand
$-(a+x) = -a + -x$, and apply the max-plus law at $(-a,-b,-c)$.
\end{proof}

\section{\texttt{number\_theory\_decide}: a sound finite-case closer}

\subsection{The tactic and its soundness}

\texttt{number\_theory\_decide} is the ordered disjunction
\[
\texttt{first}\ |\ \texttt{omega}\ |\ \texttt{decide}\ |\ \texttt{norm\_num}\ |\ (\texttt{intro x; fin\_cases x <;> decide}).
\]
In a variant tuned for residue goals \texttt{decide} precedes \texttt{norm\_num},
because \texttt{norm\_num} may partially rewrite a $\ZMod$ residue and stall whereas
\texttt{decide} evaluates it directly.

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of \texttt{number\_theory\_decide}]
Every goal closed by \texttt{number\_theory\_decide} is true.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\texttt{first}\ $|\ t_1\ |\ \dots\ |\ t_n$ closes a goal iff some $t_i$ does, so
soundness follows from soundness of each branch, and \texttt{omega},
\texttt{decide}, \texttt{norm\_num}, and \texttt{fin\_cases}+\texttt{decide} are each
sound.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Soundness sampler, \texttt{number\_theory\_decide\_sound}]
Each of the following is true and closed by the tactic: $561$ is not prime; $17$ is
prime; $\gcd(561,560)=1$; $(3-1)\mid 560$, $(11-1)\mid 560$, $(17-1)\mid 560$; and
$F_3 \equiv 0$, $F_4 \equiv 1 \pmod 2$.
\end{proposition}

The divisibility triple is Korselt's criterion for $561 = 3\cdot 11\cdot 17$, the
smallest Carmichael number.

\subsection{Reduction A --- induction with a finite base}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{two\_pow\_gt\_sq}]
For all $n \ge 5$, $n^2 < 2^n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. Values below $5$ and the base $n=5$ are closed by
\texttt{number\_theory\_decide}. For the step, from $k^2 < 2^k$ and $k \ge 5$,
$(k+1)^2 \le k^2 + k^2 < 2^k + 2^k = 2^{k+1}$, where $(k+1)^2 \le 2k^2$ is a
nonlinear estimate.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Reduction B --- Fermat congruences via $\ZMod\,p$}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{fermat\_little\_five}, \texttt{fermat\_little\_seven}]
For every integer $n$ and $p \in \{5,7\}$, $p \mid n^p - n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
It suffices (by \texttt{ZMod.intCast\_zmod\_eq\_zero\_iff\_dvd}) to show
$(n^p - n) \equiv 0$ in $\ZMod\,p$. By the ring-hom cast this reduces to the finite
identity $x^p = x$ for all $x \in \ZMod\,p$, which the tactic verifies by exhausting
the $p$ residues.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{cube\_sub\_self\_six}]
For every integer $n$, $6 \mid n^3 - n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Same pattern with the composite modulus: check $x^3 = x$ for all $x \in \ZMod\,6$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Reduction C --- Pisano periodicity by paired induction}

\begin{theorem}[Pisano step, paired form, \texttt{fib\_pisano\_step}]
Fix $m,p$ with $F_p \equiv 0$ and $F_{p+1} \equiv 1 \pmod m$. Then for every $n$,
\begin{equation}
F_{n+p} \equiv F_n \quad\text{and}\quad F_{n+p+1} \equiv F_{n+1} \pmod m. \tag{4.1}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$, carrying both congruences simultaneously. For $n=0$ they are the
seed hypotheses. For the step, the first congruence at $k+1$ is the second at $k$
after reindexing $k+1+p = (k+p)+1$; the second uses the recurrence: with
$k+1+p+1 = (k+p)+2$,
\[
F_{(k+p)+2} = F_{(k+p)+1} + F_{k+p} \equiv F_{k+1} + F_k = F_{k+2} \pmod m.
\]
A single-track induction fails because the recurrence couples consecutive terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pisano periodicity, \texttt{fib\_mod\_periodic}]
Under the seeds above, $F_{n+p} \equiv F_n \pmod m$ for all $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Concrete periods]
\texttt{fib\_mod\_two\_period}: $F_{n+3} \equiv F_n \pmod 2$;
\texttt{fib\_mod\_three\_period}: $F_{n+8} \equiv F_n \pmod 3$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the previous corollary with seeds discharged by the tactic:
$F_3 \equiv 0,\ F_4 \equiv 1 \pmod 2$ and $F_8 = 21 \equiv 0,\ F_9 = 34 \equiv 1
\pmod 3$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Bridge: Cassini modulo $m$}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{cassini\_mod}]
For all $m,n$, in $\ZMod\,m$, $F_{n+2}\,F_n - F_{n+1}^2 = (-1)^{n+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Cast the integer Cassini identity $F_{n+2}F_n - F_{n+1}^2 = (-1)^{n+1}$ along the
ring homomorphism $\Z \to \ZMod\,m$.
\end{proof}

A concrete instance (\texttt{cassini\_mod\_example}): modulo $5$ at $n=4$,
$F_6 F_4 - F_5^2 = 8\cdot 3 - 25 = -1 = (-1)^5$, is closed directly by the tactic.

\section{\texttt{spectral\_bound}: a sound eigenvalue-magnitude estimator}

\subsection{The correctness certificate}

\begin{theorem}[Weak Gershgorin row-sum bound, \texttt{eigenvalue\_rowsum\_bound}]
Let $n \ge 1$, $M \in \R^{n\times n}$, $v \in \R^n$ with $v \neq 0$, and
$\lambda \in \R$ with $Mv = \lambda v$. If $B \in \R$ satisfies
$\sum_j |M_{ij}| \le B$ for every row $i$, then $|\lambda| \le B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $i$ maximizing $|v_i|$ (possible: $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ is finite, nonempty).
Since $v \neq 0$, some $v_k \neq 0$, so $0 < |v_k| \le |v_i|$; thus $|v_i| > 0$. The
$i$-th row of $Mv = \lambda v$ reads $\sum_j M_{ij}v_j = \lambda v_i$. Then
\[
|\lambda|\,|v_i| = |\lambda v_i| = \Big|\sum_j M_{ij}v_j\Big| \le \sum_j |M_{ij}|\,|v_j|
\le \Big(\sum_j |M_{ij}|\Big)|v_i| \le B\,|v_i|,
\]
using the triangle inequality, $|v_j| \le |v_i|$, and the row bound. Cancel the
positive factor $|v_i|$.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis $v \neq 0$ is load-bearing: a zero ``eigenvector'' would admit any
$\lambda$. Taking $B := \max_i \sum_j |M_{ij}|$ recovers the sharp form.

\subsection{The tactic and its soundness}

\texttt{spectral\_bound} reduces an eigenvalue-magnitude goal $|\lambda| \le B$ to
the hypotheses of the certificate and discharges the side goals from context.

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of \texttt{spectral\_bound}]
Every goal closed by \texttt{spectral\_bound} is true, as it only applies the proved
certificate.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Worked example and real-eigenvalue corollary}

For $M = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 2\\ 0 & 3\end{pmatrix}$ both absolute row sums equal
$3$, so every eigenvalue satisfies $|\lambda| \le 3$ (the true eigenvalues are $1$
and $3$).

\begin{corollary}[Symmetric interval, \texttt{eigenvalue\_interval}]
Under the hypotheses of the certificate, a real eigenvalue obeys
$-B \le \lambda \le B$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$|\lambda| \le B \iff -B \le \lambda \le B$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion: a design principle for sound automation}

Across all three tactics the same architecture recurs. \emph{Soundness is cheap and
structural}: build the tactic only from sound parts, so ``the tactic never lies'' is
a one-line argument. \emph{Scope honesty}: each tactic has a precise boundary ---
\texttt{tropical\_simp} normalizes but cannot case-split on unknown orders;
\texttt{number\_theory\_decide} decides finite goals but not the surrounding
induction or quotient; \texttt{spectral\_bound} gives the weak (single-disc)
Gershgorin bound. \emph{Power comes from pairing}: the decisive mathematics is the
reduction --- the inductive scalar-fold law, the paired Pisano invariant, the
reduce-mod-$p$ quotient, the extremal-coordinate argument.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[\texttt{tropical\_simp} is complete for the min/+ fragment]
Every universally quantified identity $s=t$ between tropical polynomial expressions
over $\mathrm{Tropical}\,\Z$ whose only operations are $\oplus$ (min), $\odot$ (+),
and constants $0,1$ is closed by \texttt{tropical\_simp; omega}. After
\texttt{untrop} injectivity and unfolding, such an identity becomes a
quantifier-free formula in $(\Z,+,\min)$, exactly the decidable fragment
\texttt{omega} handles; only $\N$-scaling (powers) escapes.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Scaling is the unique obstruction]
\texttt{tropical\_simp; omega} closes a tropical identity iff it contains no genuine
exponent-dependence; every failure is inter-reducible with an instance of
$n \bullet \min(p,q) = \min(n\bullet p,\ n\bullet q)$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Paired-invariant induction mechanizes all Pisano periods]
There is a tactic \texttt{pisano\_period m} that computes the period $p$ via
\texttt{number\_theory\_decide} on candidate seeds and returns a proof of
$\forall n,\ F_{n+p} \equiv F_n \pmod m$ for every $m \ge 1$, with runtime
polynomial in $p$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Korselt's criterion is \texttt{number\_theory\_decide}-certifiable]
For every squarefree $n < N$ (fixed bound), ``$n$ is Carmichael $\iff$
$\forall$ prime $p \mid n,\ (p-1)\mid(n-1)$'' reduces, via one structural lemma plus
\texttt{number\_theory\_decide} on the finite prime/divisibility data, to a proof
with no \texttt{native\_decide}.
\end{conjecture}

For \texttt{spectral\_bound}, upgrade the single global disc to the union of per-row
Gershgorin discs, and extend the certificate to complex matrices and operator-norm
bounds.

\section{Conclusion}

We built three sound domain tactics --- \texttt{tropical\_simp},
\texttt{number\_theory\_decide}, and \texttt{spectral\_bound} --- and proved each one
cannot certify a falsehood, by structural soundness or by an applied correctness
certificate. In each case the tactic's leverage emerged from a partnership with
genuine structural mathematics: the inductive scalar-fold law, Pisano periodicity
via a paired invariant together with Fermat congruences, and the absolute-row-sum
eigenvalue bound. Sound automation for the routine, human-style reasoning for the
structural.

\end{document}

